# sb-image-create

`sb-image-create` is a local Python project for AI agents and automation harnesses that turns a story title and synopsis into matched image assets.

## Description

The project is aimed at a story/media pipeline where an agent needs to produce:
- a story cover image for an MP4 or story asset
- a related YouTube thumbnail

The tool should also expose the resolved visual direction so an agent or skill can understand how the story was translated into imagery and keep both outputs consistent.
The default behavior is one command that creates both images together.

## Installation

```bash
pip install -e .
```

This machine also has the command exposed globally as:

```bash
sb-image-create
```

So after installation, you can run it from anywhere as long as:
- the installed script location remains available on `PATH`
- `GEMINI_API_KEY` is available through environment variables or a local `.env`

## Usage

```bash
sb-image-create --help
```

Planned contract shape:

```bash
sb-image-create generate \
  --title "The Clockmaker's Debt" \
  --synopsis "A disgraced watchmaker discovers his missing daughter is trapped in a city that trades years of life as currency." \
  --dry-run \
  --json
```

Current implementation status:
- `generate` resolves the paired request, output filenames, and dimensions
- `--dry-run` and `--json` work today
- Gemini-backed paired generation works
- metadata sidecars are written next to generated assets

### Auth

The command needs `GEMINI_API_KEY`.

Supported behavior today:
- if `GEMINI_API_KEY` is exported in your shell, the command will use it
- if a `.env` file exists in the current working directory, the command will load it

Example:

```bash
export GEMINI_API_KEY="your-key-here"
sb-image-create generate --title "Example" --synopsis "Example synopsis" --dry-run
```

Or place this in a local `.env` in the directory you are running from:

```bash
GEMINI_API_KEY=your-key-here
```

Output behavior:
- default output directory is the current working directory
- use `--output-dir` to write elsewhere
- `name_root` is derived automatically from the title unless overridden with `--name-root`
- output files are:
  - `<name_root>_cover.jpg`
- `<name_root>_thumb.jpg`
- the thumbnail uses the story title as its default text
- the cover also includes the story title
- the thumbnail supports an optional subtitle line
- one run creates both files

Example with explicit output directory:

```bash
sb-image-create generate \
  --title "The Clockmaker's Debt" \
  --synopsis "A disgraced watchmaker discovers his missing daughter is trapped in a city that trades years of life as currency." \
  --name-root clockmakers-debt \
  --output-dir /abs/path/to/story-assets \
  --dry-run \
  --json
```

Prompt-generation behavior:
- the app uses built-in prompt logic derived from the project’s image-direction skills and agent guides
- those docs are design references for future upgrades
- the app should not need to read markdown prompt files at runtime

Config example:
- start from [`image-config.toml.example`](/Users/lee/projects/sb-image-create/image-config.toml.example)

Live generation example:

```bash
sb-image-create generate \
  --title "The Glass Orchard" \
  --synopsis "An exiled botanist returns to a frozen city where every tree has been replaced by singing glass." \
  --output-dir /abs/path/to/output \
  --json
```

Expected files after a successful run:
- `<name_root>_cover.jpg`
- `<name_root>_thumb.jpg`
- `<name_root>_metadata.json`

### Common Commands

Dry-run the resolved request:

```bash
sb-image-create generate \
  --title "The Lord of the Rings" \
  --synopsis "A humble hobbit inherits a powerful ring and joins a fellowship on a perilous journey across Middle-earth to destroy it before the Dark Lord Sauron can conquer the world." \
  --dry-run \
  --json
```

Generate real assets into the current directory:

```bash
sb-image-create generate \
  --title "The Lord of the Rings" \
  --synopsis "A humble hobbit inherits a powerful ring and joins a fellowship on a perilous journey across Middle-earth to destroy it before the Dark Lord Sauron can conquer the world." \
  --json
```

Generate real assets into a specific folder:

```bash
sb-image-create generate \
  --title "The Lord of the Rings" \
  --synopsis "A humble hobbit inherits a powerful ring and joins a fellowship on a perilous journey across Middle-earth to destroy it before the Dark Lord Sauron can conquer the world." \
  --output-dir /abs/path/to/output \
  --json
```

Generate with an explicit thumbnail subtitle:

```bash
sb-image-create generate \
  --title "The Lord of the Rings" \
  --synopsis "A humble hobbit inherits a powerful ring and joins a fellowship on a perilous journey across Middle-earth to destroy it before the Dark Lord Sauron can conquer the world." \
  --subtitle "One ring. One journey. One last chance." \
  --output-dir /abs/path/to/output \
  --json
```

### Notes

- `name_root` defaults to a filename-safe slug of the title
- title text appears on both the cover and the thumbnail
- thumbnail text defaults to the story title
- `--subtitle` adds a second line to the thumbnail when provided
- metadata includes provider, models, prompts, direction, and output paths
- Gemini may return image dimensions that differ from the requested contract dimensions; today the app records the requested contract and saves the returned image as-is

## Development Setup

Install with development dependencies:

```bash
pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

Run tests:

```bash
PYTHONPATH=src pytest
```

Format code:

```bash
black src/
ruff check src/
```

## Updating Templates

To pull the latest AgentFlow templates into this project without overwriting your custom data, run:

```bash
init-agent --update
```

This will automatically detect the Python profile and refresh only the contract files: `AGENTS.md` and `skills/*`. Living project-memory files such as `context.md` and `WHERE_AM_I.md` are preserved.

